+    <section class="journal" id="journal" aria-labelledby="journal-title">
+      <article class="journal-body" id="journal-text">
+        <h2 id="journal-title">Roaster's Journal</h2>
+        <p class="byline">On how we find, buy, and roast our beans &mdash; from the head roaster's notebook</p>
+
+        <p>Every bag we sell starts a long way from the roastery. Twice a year our sourcing team travels to the
+          growing regions we work with &mdash; the Huila and Nari&ntilde;o departments of Colombia, the Sidama and
+          Yirgacheffe zones of Ethiopia, and a small cluster of farms above 1,600 meters in Chiapas, Mexico. We go
+          during harvest, because that is the only time you can really see how a farm works: how the cherries are
+          picked, how quickly they move to processing, and how carefully the washing station sorts out the underripe
+          fruit. Those details matter more to the cup than almost anything we do back home.</p>
+
+        <h3>Relationships before contracts</h3>
+        <p>We do not buy coffee on the commodity market. Roughly eighty percent of what we roast comes from producers we
+          have worked with for at least three consecutive seasons, and several of those relationships now stretch past
+          a decade. We agree on prices before the harvest, not after, so a farmer knows what their crop is worth
+          while they are still deciding how much to invest in fertilizer, labor, and drying space. Our average price
+          paid last year was a little over 2.4 times the Fairtrade minimum, and we publish the number for every lot
+          on the back of the bag. When a cooperative has a difficult year &mdash; a late frost, a leaf rust outbreak,
+          a shipping delay that strands a container in port &mdash; we take the coffee anyway and work out the
+          quality adjustments together rather than walking away.</p>
+
+        <h3>Cupping, and cupping again</h3>
+        <p>Before any lot is approved, it is cupped at least three times: once at origin on the farm or at the dry
+          mill, once as a pre-shipment sample in our lab, and once more as an arrival sample when the container
+          lands. We score on the standard 100-point form and will not buy anything below 84, but the number is only
+          part of it. We are looking for coffees that are distinct &mdash; the bright, tea-like florals of a washed
+          Ethiopian heirloom, the heavy chocolate and dried-fruit body of a Colombian natural &mdash; and that will
+          hold those qualities for the eight to ten months we expect to be roasting them. Samples that taste
+          wonderful on day one but fade quickly do not make the cut.</p>
+
+        <h3>From green to roasted</h3>
+        <p>Green coffee is stored in GrainPro-lined bags in a climate-controlled room kept near 60&deg;F and 60
+          percent humidity, and we roast in small batches of 12 to 15 kilograms on a drum roaster so each coffee can
+          get its own profile. Lighter, washed lots get a quick, bright development; denser naturals get a slower
+          ramp to keep the sugars from scorching. Every batch is logged, and we cup one production roast a day
+          against the approved reference profile. If a roast drifts, it goes to the staff coffee pot instead of onto
+          the shelf. Nothing we sell is more than fourteen days off roast when it leaves the building.</p>
+
+        <p>None of this is the cheapest way to run a coffee company. It is, however, the only way we have found to
+          put a cup in front of you that we can honestly say we are proud of &mdash; and to make sure the people who
+          grew it are proud of it, too.</p>
+      </article>
